                "source": "Twitter Web App",
                "text": "Many of you have reached out to my office with concerns about Glenn Medical Center, and I want to take a moment to share an update on where things stand.\n\nAs you may know, Willows' own Glenn Medical Center (GMC) is facing closure after twenty-four years of having \u201cCritical Access Hospital\u201d or CAH status, which allows GMC to receive a higher reimbursement rate and fewer regulatory burdens compared to other Medicare hospitals. CAH status is Glenn Medical\u2019s lifeline for emergency healthcare in Glenn County. As the only emergency room in the county, without this status, the hospital will be forced to close its doors.\n\nThe revocation of this status is due to GMC no longer meeting the revised distance requirements as required by statute. Federal law requires a Critical Access Hospital to be over 35 miles from the next closest hospital, which in this case is Colusa Medical Center. Using I-5 and Highway 20, Glenn Medical Center is 35.7 miles away from Colusa Medical Center. However, the Center for Medicare and Medicaid Services (CMS) opted to change the calculation by using the rural cut-through of Maxwell Road, which now decreases the distance calculation to 32.5 miles, thereby putting Glenn Medical out of compliance. I am deeply frustrated about this decision, as the hospital has maintained its CAH status since 2001, and no new roads have been built, nor have the hospitals picked up and moved three miles closer during this time frame.\n\nIn 2001, Glenn Medical Center was on the brink of closure, but was saved when CMS designated it a Critical Access Hospital. CMS did not provide me with the routes used to calculate the distance requirement when GMC was first approved \u2013 CMS could not find the documentation. Then, in 2013, the Obama Administration released an Office of Inspector General report that concluded the majority of CAH-designated hospitals do not actually meet the statutory distance requirement. This resulted in the U.S. Department of Health and Human Services reviewing the status of CAH-designated hospitals, including distance requirements, for compliance purposes.\n\nToward the end of the Obama Administration and the start of the first Trump Administration in about 2016, CMS began the process of recertifying CAH-designated hospitals every three to five years to ensure they still met the mileage requirement, and that no new roads or hospitals were built nearby. In 2017, CMS recertified Glenn Medical Center as a CAH, as this was during the nearly two-year span that Colusa Medical Center was closed. With Colusa Medical closed, GMC met the distance requirement at that time because no other hospitals were within 35 miles. During the pandemic years, CMS did not take action on recertifying CAH facilities as the main priority was keeping hospitals open.\n\nThis brings us to the end of the Biden Administration in December 2024, when Glenn Medical received a notice that it could potentially lose its CAH status. After receiving reassurance from CMS officials, the hospital believed it was in the clear, as it had maintained its status for over two decades. Despite these assurances, GMC\u2019s CAH status was officially revoked in April of 2025, and its requests for mileage calculation reconsideration were denied in late August.\n\nSince April, my team and I, along with GMC representatives, have actively engaged with CMS, the U.S. Department of Health and Human Services (HHS), and the White House to find a solution that would allow Glenn Medical to stay open. HHS officials initially indicated to me that CMS would likely restore GMC\u2019s CAH status, but then abruptly changed course, saying it was constrained by the law, and a legislative fix was needed.\n\nThroughout my conversations with CMS and Administrator Oz, my biggest argument was for government accountability and fairness. On a call with Dr. Oz, CMS admitted that it should have never given Glenn Medical Center its CAH status in the first place. According to Dr. Oz, it was a mistake made by CMS 24 years ago, and there was nothing he could do to restore GMC\u2019s CAH status. Because of that \u201cmistake,\u201d now the residents of Glenn County are paying the price. This is not how government is supposed to operate \u2013 it\u2019s unfair and just plain wrong. I refuse to believe CMS\u2019s hands are tied, and I will do everything in my power to fix this situation for Glenn County.\n\nI have not given up on Glenn Medical Center and will continue to work to obtain a permanent fix on its CAH status, pursuing every option available. I am working directly with House Leadership and the White House to grant additional time before the CAH status is phased out, and have sought to include a fix in a recent bill, but it was not successful. It did put the issue on record. Simultaneously, I am asking for the Administration to set aside dollars from the $50 billion Rural Health Transformation Program, which was passed in H.R. 1, and is specifically geared to assist in keeping rural hospitals afloat. I am also working with Congressional leadership and relevant House committees to craft legislation to help restore Glenn Medical\u2019s CAH status.\n\nUnfortunately, Glenn Medical Center has announced the closure of its emergency room on September 30th as it no longer has enough staff to remain viable. California\u2019s strict staffing ratio laws also placed additional burdens on Glenn Medical\u2019s ability to keep the doors open. While Glenn Medical does not officially lose CAH reimbursement rates until April of 2026, the discouragement of staff has led to an exodus that has unfortunately outpaced the legislative process. I remain hopeful that an administrative extension of their status and a permanent legislative fix can be done this Congressional session, and we may be able to revive Glenn Medical Center.\n\nI haven\u2019t given up on Glenn Medical Center. My priority in Washington is making sure the people of Northern California are represented, and I will continue to battle for a solution.",
